# RKS - Research Kernel System

An educational Free and Open Source operating-system and kernel project for studying low-level computing through practical implementation.

## Introduction

Operating systems and low-level programming involve concepts that can be difficult to understand through theoretical study alone. Practical tools and accessible source code can complement traditional learning by allowing students to examine how these concepts are implemented.

Research Kernel System (RKS) is an educational open-source project created to support students, researchers, developers, and technology enthusiasts studying operating systems, kernels, low-level programming, and computer architecture. The project focuses on organized, practical learning through real kernel development.

## Why RKS?

RKS provides a practical environment for studying operating-system concepts through a real and independent kernel codebase. Its development involves topics such as memory and process management, scheduling, interrupts, boot processes, hardware interaction, computer architecture, C, Assembly, and kernel development.

Working directly with these mechanisms helps connect theoretical concepts with their implementation at the software and hardware boundary.

## Objective

The objective of RKS is to develop an open-source educational platform for sharing knowledge about operating systems and kernels, supporting the learning process of students, researchers, developers, and technology enthusiasts.

## Free and Open Source

RKS is a **Free and Open Source Software (FOSS)** project licensed under [**GNU General Public License v3.0**](#License). Its source code is intended to be studied, modified, shared, and improved by developers, students, researchers, and other contributors.

The project's philosophy and educational direction were inspired by the **GNU Project**, particularly its emphasis on studying source code, modifying software, collaboration, and sharing knowledge. RKS is not affiliated with the GNU Project or the Free Software Foundation.

## Project Status

RKS is under active development. Its architecture, features, documentation, and compatibility may change as the project evolves.

## Contributing

Contributions from students, developers, researchers, operating-system enthusiasts, and low-level programming enthusiasts are welcome. Contributions may include bug fixes, kernel improvements, documentation, tests, code cleanup, issue reports, and technical discussions.

### Before submitting code

Follow the project's existing code style and formatting configuration:

```text
.indent.pro
.clang-format
```

It's likable keeping changes focused and avoid unrelated modifications in the same pull request. Follow the existing code style, explain implementation decisions, test changes before submitting them, and reference the relevant issue when needed.

